    sha256: e87cd0079de36002a9ae5d065256197eccce3b9257537a29da5bd3220145021a
  body: "# Evidence of age, marriage and death in the DMG\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe chapter describes
    evidence needed to establish age, marriage or death where those facts matter to a benefit, including help to
    obtain or verify the evidence.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nThis is evidential guidance, not a universal definition
    of identity or a replacement for civil-registration law. Benefit, event, territory and the actual question determine
    the needed proof; age evidence alone does not establish Pension Credit entitlement.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\
    \n- [DMG 10000, PDF page 2](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/681c8caa9ef97b58cce3e5a4/dmgch10.pdf#page=2)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 10: Evidence of age, marriage and death .\n- [DMG 10001, PDF page 2](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/681c8caa9ef97b58cce3e5a4/dmgch10.pdf#page=2)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 10: Evidence of age, marriage and death .\n- [DMG 10002, PDF page 2](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/681c8caa9ef97b58cce3e5a4/dmgch10.pdf#page=2)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 10: Evidence of age, marriage and death .\n- [DMG 10003, PDF page 2](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/681c8caa9ef97b58cce3e5a4/dmgch10.pdf#page=2)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 10: Evidence of age, marriage and death .\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted
    interpretation of the 15 September 2026 source snapshot; unreviewed and requiring specialist review. The source
    is DWP guidance, not a consolidated statement of legislation. This is an independent experimental publication,
    not an official DWP document, an entitlement decision or an executable benefits rule.\n\nPDF page numbers are
    one-based file positions. Machine extraction can alter reading order, footnotes and characters. Quoted relationships
    preserve extracted text; no visual or specialist acceptance is claimed. Source dates, legal effects and current
    applicability remain separate review tasks.\n\n- `dmg-vol3-ch10` PDF SHA-256: `e87cd0079de36002a9ae5d065256197eccce3b9257537a29da5bd3220145021a`;
    extracted pages SHA-256: `0fdc816b2a57b058f8826c36ac756cd414da56c94f580a9b5b32e0ff874a68b8`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-burden-of-proof
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source places proof on the claimant or representative while explicitly requiring assistance
      in obtaining it.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch10/0002
      quote: "10002 The onus of providing satisfactory proof of age, marriage or death is on the claimant or their\n\
        \nrepresentative1. However, claimants or their representatives should be helped as much as possible by\n\
        \n\n1. advising the claimant about the kind of evidence that may be needed and\n\n\n2. advising how to obtain
        that evidence and\n\n\n3. undertaking searches to verify the events or dates in question.\n\n\n        \
        \                                                                         1 SS (C&P) Regs, reg 7(1)"
      locator: DMG 10002
  - target: term/qualifying-age
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source cross-refers to Pension Credit qualifying age in a particular JSA example; the
      relation is contextual and does not state a current age threshold.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch10/0002
      quote: "10001 With other benefits a question arises only in limited circumstances, for example, a claimant
        in\nreceipt of JSA(IB) has to provide evidence of age when nearing the qualifying age for SPC so that PP
        can\n\nbe considered1.\n\n\nNote: See DMG Chapter 77 for guidance on the qualifying age for SPC.\n\n\n \
        \                                                                         1 JSA Regs, reg 83 & 84 & Sch
        1"
      locator: DMG 10001
  - target: term/dmg-civil-status-primary-evidence
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The chapter identifies statutory register copies as its primary evidence route.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch10/0005
      quote: "10030 The best evidence of age, marriage or death that can be provided is a certified copy of an entry\n\
        which has by law to be made in a register concerning the event. The certificates are issued by\n\n\n1. the
        Registrar General or\n\n\n2. a Superintendent Registrar or\n\n\n3. a Registrar of Births and Deaths or\n\
        \n\n4. a Registrar of Marriages or\n\n\n5. in Scotland, the Registrar General for Scotland or a District
        Registrar or\n\n\n6. a member of the clergy of the Church of England or\n\n\n7. a Roman Catholic priest
        who is authorized to make an entry of marriage in a Register of Marriages 1 or\n\n\n8. a person authorized
        to keep a register of marriages2 for example a Secretary of a Synagogue or a\nRegistering Officer of the
        Society of Friends having the custody of the Register in which the event is\nrecorded or\n\n\n9. a Marriage
        Officer3.\n\n\nNote 1: The certificate of a Marriage Officer relates to marriage of a British subject before
        a British\nambassador, consul or other official on one of His Majesty’s ships in foreign stations or within
        the lines of\nBritish Army or Royal Air Force units serving abroad.\n\n\nNote 2: Ecclesiastical certificates
        of Roman Catholic and Jewish marriages are not extracts from a\nstatutory register and can only be used
        as secondary evidence.\n\n\nNote 3: See DMG 10036 for guidance on secondary evidence if primary evidence
        is not available.\n\n\n                                              1 Marriage Act 49; 2 s 63; 3 Foreign
        Marriages Act 1892, s 11"
      locator: DMG 10030
  - target: term/dmg-civil-status-secondary-evidence
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The chapter supplies a route for considering secondary evidence where primary evidence is
      unavailable.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch10/0006
      quote: "10036 If primary evidence is not available, the DM should ask for the claimant’s observations before\n\
        making a decision. Unsupported secondary evidence carries less weight than primary evidence. The DM\nshould
        ensure that as much information as possible has been obtained, before considering the relative\nvalue of
        each piece of evidence."
      locator: DMG 10036
